#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179719 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179581 :question/id #uuid "d41ebc3c-f998-577e-a50b-a08faadd561f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9474" :question/problem "![](images/f9/f9de4a1c2dfb6b8939b2440930eefb8b7020947bd7b943021214adf4cbfce735.png)\n\n    \n\nWhich of the above diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y) = 2y^2-x^2?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $\\nabla f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-2x \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =-2x\\,i+4y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(2,1),$ $(-2,1),$ and $(1,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $\\nabla f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(2,1) & =-2\\cdot 2\\,i+4\\cdot 1\\,j=-4\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-2,1) & =-2\\cdot (-2)\\,i+4\\cdot 1\\,j=4\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,-1) & =-2\\cdot 1\\,i+4\\cdot (-1)\\,j=-2\\,i-4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors at their corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/b8/b88c62b154cecaace206248b62f72b8aa1f71662c0427adfbbabd5c2b4738a6b.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a43f82e-6682-56af-994a-b0bdf12b4095" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048917 :topic/math-academy-id 3344 :topic/title "Visualizing Vector Fields"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179966 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4e4f1e4-9db8-5103-b0e7-6bfa97069629" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/56/565dab4b729e587b63826dc9f39363eaf0e941b39188f745b71a336368ed0b4d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177466} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d9eaab-74ea-5160-8a14-8de7f5af555c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e8/e863d2283ea97745102ab489bd7721ad8987c37f33b246023bb2549053f3ebf7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177467} {:answer/id #uuid "1cdb37f0-b0be-50ac-906c-f6836336a177" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2a/2abd84e2cd0c55cdec95ccb21c8a75270b97d6b7a3bf8b80137e38ab198c24c6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177468} {:answer/id #uuid "3667299f-b2ef-5465-9b35-0ad04230adb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/dc/dc36c0bfd1e089dc4e5017b4d8eb3fc342aff35ca6b8ce1943a209915592d83c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177469} {:answer/id #uuid "71859476-7f1d-5d93-8176-0d32012095b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7d/7d9612ef402244aad3a71fb8f8de587eb4be546ad3c1718885c1bafbb1ae1066.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177470}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5efd63b9-13ac-5b6c-ab07-8031398f0949" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177465} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2294730-1a63-56a9-975a-ed773eb5c601" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7d/7d9612ef402244aad3a71fb8f8de587eb4be546ad3c1718885c1bafbb1ae1066.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387182} {:answer/id #uuid "354411d0-1865-5b34-bf67-4f56be47754e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/dc/dc36c0bfd1e089dc4e5017b4d8eb3fc342aff35ca6b8ce1943a209915592d83c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387183} {:answer/id #uuid "311b14fe-ee07-5a20-a6a5-59a701d67acb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/56/565dab4b729e587b63826dc9f39363eaf0e941b39188f745b71a336368ed0b4d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387184} {:answer/id #uuid "0d3a365b-59f8-5560-9234-ece8c5e30b6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e8/e863d2283ea97745102ab489bd7721ad8987c37f33b246023bb2549053f3ebf7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387185} {:answer/id #uuid "b7e37acb-40d4-5fed-9de7-438853dac171" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2a/2abd84e2cd0c55cdec95ccb21c8a75270b97d6b7a3bf8b80137e38ab198c24c6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "354411d0-1865-5b34-bf67-4f56be47754e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/dc/dc36c0bfd1e089dc4e5017b4d8eb3fc342aff35ca6b8ce1943a209915592d83c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387183} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ac480a4-ae34-526d-b6ff-99c48416aa4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387181}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b5a8a53e-8c38-522a-bf60-158bb8a0f977" :question/math-academy-id "q-122407" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y) \\\\  & =2x \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-y) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i-j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,1),$ $(0,2),$ and $(2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,1) & =2\\cdot (-2)\\,i-j=-4\\,i-j \\\\ ∇f(0,2) & =2\\cdot (0)\\,i-j=-\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =2\\cdot 2\\,i-j=4\\,i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/dc/dc36c0bfd1e089dc4e5017b4d8eb3fc342aff35ca6b8ce1943a209915592d83c.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179973 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ba90d78-1161-52a4-93c0-d995aaaf7e51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6c/6cf967e8a5d3bc7b2ac021414875f5221ef52790dfbdd6e214cccc7f0e8be2d2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387299} {:answer/id #uuid "bb982230-23d8-541a-a311-793fcd335e70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4b/4be9586aaa886ccfe092f93c106a5f7233509c798c9a90ecfda0363173dc7afb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387300} {:answer/id #uuid "6f167c07-25dc-5f9e-956f-2fe0933e7ed6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/95/9593ba00b964194c1d91549fe38a1334a478ffd6b724534c4512a11d19d683aa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387301} {:answer/id #uuid "640a700e-3b7c-59e8-83e6-b88bdc953d51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f8/f814f8c7e6d879cbe25e5108704e29464d0c39859371ee4fac3bfb3e72f90696.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387302} {:answer/id #uuid "09b9b72e-d802-5315-b38d-ee456018cf2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/26/26a5c0123d93231a800456e9f06f6ba7450861838c87d4d1b3ae77dab4cec6cf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09b9b72e-d802-5315-b38d-ee456018cf2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/26/26a5c0123d93231a800456e9f06f6ba7450861838c87d4d1b3ae77dab4cec6cf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387303} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7fe3fc1-2c7d-5b68-96b7-12857eae01aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387298}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe3ebbef-9b0f-50cb-ab18-6df5f7b1d83c" :question/math-academy-id "q-122789" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{y}^{2}}{4}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{y}^{2}}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{y}^{2}}{4}) \\\\  & =-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}\\,i-y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,2),$ $(2,-3),$ and $(2,1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,2) & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot (-2)^{2}\\,i-2\\,j=3\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-3) & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {2}^{2}\\,i-(-3)\\,j=3\\,i+3\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {2}^{2}\\,i-\\,j=3\\,i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/26/26a5c0123d93231a800456e9f06f6ba7450861838c87d4d1b3ae77dab4cec6cf.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256697 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f1e18b0-1117-563b-8441-3490065e61e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d3/d3fc823cfcea400219e553f9e6911a312f0a14c7f39aafb49d1af3463cb3e46b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253939} {:answer/id #uuid "46e3f6a4-7353-56bc-8961-cc92fd971024" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0e/0ea540eb4a48d1fb1777e2d6397e6d38937e9795c4d810b48c0fda0801d1c9ba.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254269} {:answer/id #uuid "64b24222-51a7-5272-9ca7-2db93a07e9b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ba/ba42a974a78df2c0d228e8f5ae543019841c8a7b47e38f02e139fe2d97b8ecf5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254572} {:answer/id #uuid "8ad5cb04-3d21-55c7-a532-d1bd2ca52b4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f4/f4f0b2e96fa7ee0f1dca7d57a2815e93d77665c0285fa81f2a8b2c5965a39415.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254895} {:answer/id #uuid "bde88cfd-9cb5-56e6-ba40-080493eb0ad6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/36/369ebf19405768bcca19f4981f0237647836250548b0a33f9b4fdba92d85ddc9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bde88cfd-9cb5-56e6-ba40-080493eb0ad6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/36/369ebf19405768bcca19f4981f0237647836250548b0a33f9b4fdba92d85ddc9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255365} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e297b46-c5d5-5311-943f-eb3962e376bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255938}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f30aab0-000a-5430-bdac-144d62e4296d" :question/math-academy-id "q-122693" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =x \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =x\\,i+y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-1,1),$ $(2,2),$ and $(2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-1,1) & =-\\,i+j \\\\ ∇f(2,2) & =2\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =2\\,i+(-1)j=2\\,i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/36/369ebf19405768bcca19f4981f0237647836250548b0a33f9b4fdba92d85ddc9.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256701 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f4df781-1911-525f-b679-c4fc003bb1e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0f/0f45b194df67a060c195d4368050cdad9b8bfefab86b41d5eb63723631543d7b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253805} {:answer/id #uuid "4e60cd36-67ef-5e43-b4e0-2891d7e90e88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/92/92f46798ebeae9017c91ddae24e0f68cef81a1045314950bc4b56af003af0b42.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254344} {:answer/id #uuid "ca078818-e709-5a55-8db7-62d422c4af5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6c/6c76d43daa86b2c8034e3f12592ee6c18bc533034cb255934ff7eb08b2d53623.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255466} {:answer/id #uuid "f4d5cca5-5d32-550a-85a6-767c2bc17710" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/df/df6472974d246217341c69163c2336190980b5aacaa5b1a5c11cbb7126ddde86.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255815} {:answer/id #uuid "ff3b9331-f12b-5cc1-9824-f87b6a349f2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/76/7618ae208ba53f12fb31988a3e0d0cb40f78ddc4348c5049bb09127039c4d965.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e60cd36-67ef-5e43-b4e0-2891d7e90e88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/92/92f46798ebeae9017c91ddae24e0f68cef81a1045314950bc4b56af003af0b42.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254344} :answer-field/id #uuid "7db82da5-3e98-5b0c-942d-8b5d0515f72a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256155}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e2b6964a-0bdc-5f5e-bdc8-f351c47c4755" :question/math-academy-id "q-122699" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=2y-x?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y-x) \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y-x) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =-i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,2),$ $(2,2),$ and $(2,-2).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,2) & =-i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,2) & =-i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-2) & =-i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/92/92f46798ebeae9017c91ddae24e0f68cef81a1045314950bc4b56af003af0b42.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256705 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39ead94b-aaa3-5df0-8bc4-db320602981c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1d/1da33de6ab53eee30cb7dbdba61a437fd9e3209437d061d2c546386981962942.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254178} {:answer/id #uuid "5e8a3268-82d7-5922-85eb-904111ce9e5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ab/abdb12ae2b6a67283ae65485e124b2a5264ab63a401e8993829f8b56704af479.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254512} {:answer/id #uuid "5fc84add-7dd1-5a0b-9987-6f006015797c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5e/5e19f0874cbbfcf99c45951c98b19d941c39451c33887fbdcacdb75faeffdc57.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254527} {:answer/id #uuid "88fda897-7ef2-5696-9746-cc266403903e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1e/1e9b6c550e79abfd03d4ab0c0c7942b5185d24fd2e72203d1cb87c0895e3a6f4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254886} {:answer/id #uuid "c0c76fbf-13a6-5b8f-be2d-44312d2dbaa9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/82/8270942dbaed9f5177e179104bb479802a39c48afa2a528b138b458c042a1556.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39ead94b-aaa3-5df0-8bc4-db320602981c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1d/1da33de6ab53eee30cb7dbdba61a437fd9e3209437d061d2c546386981962942.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254178} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9de488d-3ab0-5329-8a1a-ee5aeb6ecbc8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256253}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f32efae7-0f28-5e13-b999-daa26821b418" :question/math-academy-id "q-122713" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(xy)?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-1,1),$ $(0,-2),$ and $(2,0).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-1,1) & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{(-1)^{2}(1)^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)}{(-1)^{2}(1)^{2}+1}\\,j=i-j \\\\ ∇f(0,-2) & =\\frac{2\\cdot (-2)}{(0)^{2}(-2)^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{2\\cdot 0}{(0)^{2}(-2)^{2}+1}\\,j=-4\\,i \\\\ ∇f(2,0) & =\\frac{2\\cdot 0}{(2)^{2}(0)^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{2\\cdot 2}{(2)^{2}(0)^{2}+1}\\,j=4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1d/1da33de6ab53eee30cb7dbdba61a437fd9e3209437d061d2c546386981962942.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256708 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b07ebbb-ae2a-5805-a7b7-426af5bcb497" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/13/135122e85da455699e45c1e276152b20fee0ac9d18441eb5620b1aa43654c81b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254314} {:answer/id #uuid "8fca9e4f-70ee-50c3-8e6c-d0cd30d777a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/25/2595c11a1b30fee9d76028010d2c7e608250c0d48ce0191d7da2a5412af6a110.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254930} {:answer/id #uuid "cc2f9076-3f65-544d-b6ed-94bd3f9e2b0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e4/e4f9011d4497ac01495c1dcde81d9ba19e9b48f9d78c36ca6259d3a2465983b7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255484} {:answer/id #uuid "dba6b19f-483e-5557-90ff-71eb95e4575c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3b/3bf6311586ae40bb490414108bbf232f40e1e727da45bbdbe3123e60c17f6268.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255617} {:answer/id #uuid "e36ece5b-4544-54b9-8906-c7cdc5544ad4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/71/716f99a985d8b4909e76f922fc3bf5b3350f9cb7496ae0a0a429421016a19267.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fca9e4f-70ee-50c3-8e6c-d0cd30d777a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/25/2595c11a1b30fee9d76028010d2c7e608250c0d48ce0191d7da2a5412af6a110.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254930} :answer-field/id #uuid "fefeda5b-1048-597b-a49a-96c4ffaa9319" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256366}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7de89f2b-b3a4-52f5-bd83-f755a3097808" :question/math-academy-id "q-122738" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{(y-2x)^{2}}{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{(y-2x)^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-2(y-2x) \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{(y-2x)^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =y-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =-2(y-2x)\\,i+(y-2x)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(1,1),$ $(1,3),$ and $(-2,-3).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(1,1) & =-2\\cdot (1-2\\cdot 1)\\,i+(1-2\\cdot 1)\\,j=2\\,i-j \\\\ ∇f(1,3) & =-2\\cdot (3-2\\cdot 1)\\,i+(3-2\\cdot 1)\\,j=-2\\,i+j \\\\ ∇f(-2,-3) & =-2\\cdot (-3-2\\cdot (-2))\\,i+(-3-2\\cdot (-2))\\,j=-2\\,i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/25/2595c11a1b30fee9d76028010d2c7e608250c0d48ce0191d7da2a5412af6a110.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383006 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8bec6eb-8e0a-5ce5-966f-3cfc2e175f0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fd/fdcd6cebad2e9aef26daf2987b2890a1b05ceeab5f8e803b631cdb4a6a4598c4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383008} {:answer/id #uuid "eee84ab8-4f31-5f75-abfa-e9d0d3f0fc7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d9/d91a13ce4ee54923d33ab5df7506ce8200d02f53695d85d186e0c9f5dc9345db.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383009} {:answer/id #uuid "8f0f9738-4804-5496-8f1c-2a371d83d08f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/57/57ab7ae667788fe307f67df7078d91ee8c0e82f9703d1c59c439a790af9b2fbb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383010} {:answer/id #uuid "837b5ab1-a135-59d4-bd04-ec207c823e91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/22/2215b79d50f88933aa2b83eb76251eb97a9f3491ffc86b0c5e0eba3debd80cdd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383011} {:answer/id #uuid "c6be86a5-a237-5718-8d27-339f9839dbc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/94/94f8ffc9b7e98188d2a0429ada7f936df7816e9a04d42e97d3b57e6ab34cfe41.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8bec6eb-8e0a-5ce5-966f-3cfc2e175f0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fd/fdcd6cebad2e9aef26daf2987b2890a1b05ceeab5f8e803b631cdb4a6a4598c4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383008} :answer-field/id #uuid "f519e720-525e-5a35-9830-b680df8d1973" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383007}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b63c956-bea1-5b1b-a3bb-61d243984ef7" :question/math-academy-id "q-122687" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =x \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =x\\,i-y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,1),$ $(1,1),$ and $(2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,1) & =(-2)\\,i-j=-2\\,i-j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =i-j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =2\\,i-(-1)\\,j=2i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/fd/fdcd6cebad2e9aef26daf2987b2890a1b05ceeab5f8e803b631cdb4a6a4598c4.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383013 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1deaab34-479d-5252-a2e1-4b9b7d0982eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/73/736cd2cf5d297363c4546465ef46476107cab0e5ec12de51d1792df827dc6c16.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383015} {:answer/id #uuid "096acd8c-d44b-588e-a2a6-4c831113ad73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1b/1b7d5a942e7a4df8f0b1e71051fa527213d2e43abaf411bc5024c66f90208e92.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383016} {:answer/id #uuid "761f05d0-5903-5c2f-900e-e1fb72b55741" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8e/8efbe2acdfe8530f68a51bf4217c2d7689c491f3a8a412e6f3777cb12932e0fa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383017} {:answer/id #uuid "bf0d60eb-ac2d-5679-8076-b7429123e536" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/23/235d03d87913ebe00b51a0643920c9565e8ffcb88ab32c63bf47ce3b39429de0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383018} {:answer/id #uuid "98acb1ee-bc76-53a3-80b8-02da68d6b4eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/59/59c2db214327f1e078c0ddc5770e91db5e3de021b9e16fbf0ae7a29545a633d1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98acb1ee-bc76-53a3-80b8-02da68d6b4eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/59/59c2db214327f1e078c0ddc5770e91db5e3de021b9e16fbf0ae7a29545a633d1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383019} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ec7584e-69da-56ab-a5cb-c7e9ee2cc549" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383014}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "412a3f65-3326-5eb4-8063-61b8d30c5ab0" :question/math-academy-id "q-122708" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{2}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}+{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{2}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & ={2}^{x} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{2}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & ={2}^{x}\\,i+2y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,1),$ $(0,1),$ and $(2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,1) & ={2}^{-2}\\,i+2\\cdot (1)\\,j=\\frac{1}{4}\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(0,1) & ={2}^{0}\\,i+2\\cdot 1\\,j=i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & ={2}^{2}\\,i+2\\cdot (-1)\\,j=4\\,i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/59/59c2db214327f1e078c0ddc5770e91db5e3de021b9e16fbf0ae7a29545a633d1.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387263 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c7d1d36-652e-5181-9302-abbb5df8798b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/14/1461b0737bf83fee8e16c1e5e102c36c2042e46935550ebc08a0d5774a561cd3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387265} {:answer/id #uuid "293009e5-bb46-5343-920e-66801c6cfbce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ac/acb2066428e0660e7d4e106acd5faf60600d6f93de0b6cf4c43ee2b5864e6e51.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387266} {:answer/id #uuid "7be00e9c-596e-5c63-ac00-60deba93c44a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0d/0d5f1fb1b13459937f7bd3ff0ed8b6da641fcd346dc961d7ec5b98be29e45b1d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387267} {:answer/id #uuid "fa9fdbaf-e58e-56ff-981f-39b0969f23ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cd/cdebaf3d8c07fe6cb26c4fb2c5b72d2bd60dc968b3345fc1c4a5737d8c6c2b53.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387268} {:answer/id #uuid "e227d23f-1359-5012-8c53-e4feec2f7c4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8a/8a35641fa57b01a95a0c1d5b3dba8a0072b291a6822ac7b2f5e0473b9037796b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c7d1d36-652e-5181-9302-abbb5df8798b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/14/1461b0737bf83fee8e16c1e5e102c36c2042e46935550ebc08a0d5774a561cd3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387265} :answer-field/id #uuid "07a54f60-4401-5cf2-ba14-b1330ea47fc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387264}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "40689dc7-e924-59ea-9bdd-9f2acdedd9ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-122619" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=x+{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\,i+2y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-4,1),$ $(1,1),$ and $(3,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-4,1) & =i+2\\cdot 1\\,j=i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =i+2\\cdot 1\\,j=i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(3,-1) & =i+2\\cdot (-1)\\,j=i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/14/1461b0737bf83fee8e16c1e5e102c36c2042e46935550ebc08a0d5774a561cd3.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387270 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bec373fa-27cc-5734-871a-ec77749ea6a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0c/0c425b6fcb88f4c958a7c0371a64a815eda5ebf8ed14b0adc06875de1e24003b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387272} {:answer/id #uuid "6120694d-f5d6-5a30-a9b1-04836470b4fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/af/af7fdf48d36f477174867761b90d9349344029980012f1f7907f544efd3893bc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387273} {:answer/id #uuid "5738a2a6-58f2-5cb8-befa-f76d6d0fb2c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/48/48c57f24904442c74178682db34aaa0113eb84042c78516a4e6f961e73b0f9eb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387274} {:answer/id #uuid "11eee978-2cbc-57da-8a9a-9ffddf6422c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/02/020c3ec7cd0e5e57bb4c6de8516988187c9038d9e5598b116966b71fa8b917d8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387275} {:answer/id #uuid "04551b05-601c-557a-a5a2-69cba4463197" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/25/25ed4b4750662433bf4405b5e63ab78396688a985c76e2d34d449c0689830380.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387276}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11eee978-2cbc-57da-8a9a-9ffddf6422c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/02/020c3ec7cd0e5e57bb4c6de8516988187c9038d9e5598b116966b71fa8b917d8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387275} :answer-field/id #uuid "c39f6b60-6db1-56ec-93e1-e634b438a208" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387271}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "14b7ceda-a408-53c9-aeb5-05da06c84fba" :question/math-academy-id "q-122732" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & ={y}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\,i+2xy\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,1),$ $(2,1),$ and $(-2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,1) & ={1}^{2}\\,i+2\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 1\\,j=i-4j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & ={1}^{2}\\,i+2\\cdot 2\\cdot 1\\,j=i+4\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-2,-1) & =(-1)^{2}\\,i+2\\cdot (-2)\\cdot (-1)\\,j=i+4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/02/020c3ec7cd0e5e57bb4c6de8516988187c9038d9e5598b116966b71fa8b917d8.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424190 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0afc3cbd-80cd-5f79-95d1-b02b58d14af7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ce/ce7690cdb4a4da4841dc7f551b73d379945141964de4f9de00dbe6a39a6e36e8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424184} {:answer/id #uuid "25ed12c7-2e43-5b16-b2e3-173b3c5367b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e2/e239433cc1c62333506d14eac022488f2c6786837f17f20e6a1fbba9f25dbf12.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424185} {:answer/id #uuid "99f6e268-33a9-5e9a-a609-e6ee2c4c38d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f6/f6264905bceebe59d764a88f8566dc5eb21237dd28ae5c2052fbd3c766ba6c14.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424186} {:answer/id #uuid "c5dba748-9027-5a4a-bf33-0feb67920158" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4c/4c5c4b297d1aaaa672628f0621e8ed71fbacedd9f418d7d7068da8cba5847315.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424187} {:answer/id #uuid "cad5833b-0aa5-5948-b9b9-b322bb2fcf7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/96/96851fd456d3b9f0a6af941a255926f35e29bbe778ed05556af3f69145e38ef2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cad5833b-0aa5-5948-b9b9-b322bb2fcf7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/96/96851fd456d3b9f0a6af941a255926f35e29bbe778ed05556af3f69145e38ef2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424188} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9f1a53d-9e00-54ab-b914-f5c8b1d4efa0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424189}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6d9aa2a3-1ea1-5a5e-9577-ca5789b6206d" :question/math-academy-id "q-122737" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=(x-y)^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-y)^{2}) \\\\  & =2(x-y) \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x-y)^{2}) \\\\  & =-2(x-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =2(x-y)\\,i-2(x-y)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(2,1),$ $(1,2),$ and $(1,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(2,1) & =2\\cdot (2-1)\\,i-2\\cdot (2-1)\\,j=2\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,2) & =2\\cdot (1-2)\\,i-2\\cdot (1-2)\\,j=-2\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,-1) & =2\\cdot (1-(-1))\\,i-2\\cdot (1-(-1))\\,j=4\\,i-4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/96/96851fd456d3b9f0a6af941a255926f35e29bbe778ed05556af3f69145e38ef2.png)"}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Gradient Vector Field of a Function"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179728 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179590 :question/id #uuid "9d13f401-5e07-5b07-8916-df2b0b6b8f74" :question/math-academy-id "e-9515" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overline{AB}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to the point $B(1, 1),$ calculate the line integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C x^2y\\,\\mathrm{d}x + xy\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a} = 0\\,\\mathbf{i} + 0\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b} = 1\\,\\mathbf{i} + 1\\,\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =t\\,i+t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\le t \\le 1.$ So, we have that $x(t) = t$ and $y(t) = t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,dx+xy\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\cdot t\\cdot 1+t\\cdot t\\cdot 1\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "223ec615-045d-543e-96f4-fec8882bec9e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048512 :topic/math-academy-id 3699 :topic/title "Line Integrals of Scalar Functions Over Line Segments"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050666 :topic/math-academy-id 3705 :topic/title "Sums of Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Over Parametric Curves"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180009 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cd60488-a0dc-5fbe-ad61-99ef09f53fd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257588} {:answer/id #uuid "7d18e2c3-2a49-58ba-9262-8615f99ff198" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257886} {:answer/id #uuid "7df658bc-3949-564c-8f0a-ff4d65a8122c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257897} {:answer/id #uuid "9647de0b-9ead-58ae-98b3-078246de2961" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258104} {:answer/id #uuid "f9d41240-8070-5238-af02-d0421b2f2cb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9647de0b-9ead-58ae-98b3-078246de2961" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258104} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce8f3449-3ae9-55ef-ba7f-735d655fcb1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259396}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bccb8e18-5ece-5d84-8a75-003e818045de" :question/math-academy-id "q-124654" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{MN}$ traversed from the point $M(-1,0)$ to the point $N(2,4),$ then ${\\int }_{C}y\\,dx-x\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i$ and $b=2\\,i+4\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =-i+t(2\\,i+4\\,j-(-i)) \\\\  & =-i+t(3\\,i+4\\,j) \\\\  & =(3t-1)\\,i+4t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=3t-1$ and $y(t)=4t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=3\\,i+4\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,dx-x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4t(3)-(3t-1)(4)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4\\,dt \\\\  & =4t\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4(1-0) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180284 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37eefa34-5c7b-5c18-b8ed-71a775d5e074" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348952} {:answer/id #uuid "3de4c8b4-1db8-5fc8-a86d-71a5c61c7d18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349007} {:answer/id #uuid "ab99d944-7135-5a96-8ebe-a930f4912b68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349969} {:answer/id #uuid "be137474-0788-5e5a-961f-c0c550ca9ef5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350136} {:answer/id #uuid "ec9c8ba2-744b-59f6-8a7e-28308457b79d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350516}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37eefa34-5c7b-5c18-b8ed-71a775d5e074" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348952} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7c6a695-a9f0-5e4f-ad87-ac5134afda03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351054}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5d8fdb2d-e684-54ed-a57b-9028bda74ea1" :question/math-academy-id "q-81096" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{MN}$ traversed from the point $M(0,1)$ to the point $N(3,3),$ then ${\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,dx-y\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=j$ and $b=3\\,i+3\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =j+t(3\\,i+3\\,j-j) \\\\  & =j+t(3\\,i+2\\,j) \\\\  & =3t\\,i+(1+2t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=3t$ and $y(t)=1+2t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=3\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,dx-y\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3t)^{2}\\cdot 3-(1+2t)\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}27{t}^{2}-2-4t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[9{t}^{3}-2t-2{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[9(1)^{3}-2(1)-2(1)^{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259699 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2061316d-1856-583b-a012-175c95e1c965" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257043} {:answer/id #uuid "563f3b75-047f-5df7-8de7-9221c43542f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257524} {:answer/id #uuid "721d5e3a-d79f-55f9-9018-78465894af00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257783} {:answer/id #uuid "78eb821b-6c70-554e-972c-afc5237e47d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257851} {:answer/id #uuid "a6fda23d-0a63-5c16-8aa0-61bd7f16d537" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258240}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6fda23d-0a63-5c16-8aa0-61bd7f16d537" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258240} :answer-field/id #uuid "b256fecd-a217-5f81-b776-bf372703f7c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259343}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c9d024fb-1731-5e0e-8fd1-3a79afb84b21" :question/math-academy-id "q-124652" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from the point $A(-1,2)$ to the point $B(1,0),$ then ${\\int }_{C}xy\\,dx+dy=$" :question/worked-solution "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+2\\,j$ and $b=i.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =-i+2\\,j+t(i-(-i+2\\,j)) \\\\  & =-i+2\\,j+t(2\\,i-2\\,j) \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\,i+(2-2t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=2t-1$ and $y(t)=2-2t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=2\\,i-2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy\\,dx+dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-1)(2-2t)(2)+(-2)\\,dt \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-1)(2-2t)-1\\,dt \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}-4{t}^{2}+6t-3\\,dt \\\\  & =2{[-\\frac{4{t}^{3}}{3}+3{t}^{2}-3t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2[-\\frac{4}{3}+3-3]-2[0] \\\\  & =2[-\\frac{4}{3}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259702 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "518dc48b-166a-5cab-b682-ceb513ca018f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{e}^{4}-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257475} {:answer/id #uuid "5514bb7f-2242-59f6-a527-7a456fa434c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{4}-2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257508} {:answer/id #uuid "72ddc658-03d6-5c6d-9682-1d117f5448cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{6}-3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257792} {:answer/id #uuid "d551764d-1496-5882-b891-2f87604939f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{e}^{6}-1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258650} {:answer/id #uuid "ef05dfcc-4837-55ea-a44c-b1bc4977ee48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{6}-1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef05dfcc-4837-55ea-a44c-b1bc4977ee48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{6}-1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258854} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac77208e-6c5b-5fce-9439-9602678d59af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259333}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d143a12d-610a-5ab9-bde5-0709d25859cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-124658" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to the point $B(4,2),$ then ${\\int }_{C}{e}^{x+y}\\,dx-{e}^{x+y}\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=0\\,i+0\\,j$ and $b=4\\,i+2\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =0\\,i+0\\,j+t(4\\,i+2\\,j-(0\\,i+0\\,j)) \\\\  & =4t\\,i+2t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=4t$ and $y(t)=2t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=4\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x+y}\\,dx-{e}^{x+y}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-{e}^{x+y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x+y}(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{4t+2t}(4-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{6t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{6t}}{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{6(1)}}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{6(0)}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{6}}{3}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{6}-1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259704 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08792d36-b712-50c1-b364-d2539ef8bc5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{28}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256854} {:answer/id #uuid "10c49561-a693-5a9e-8fe4-f237ac8c02dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256937} {:answer/id #uuid "26adfe2c-c9e9-5b3f-a5e0-1481d5b90994" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257116} {:answer/id #uuid "6c787fa5-b320-5415-99de-06c8e742d4f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257739} {:answer/id #uuid "6e385de7-39aa-5450-a0e9-ee6c4afd30d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08792d36-b712-50c1-b364-d2539ef8bc5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{28}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256854} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed846ac5-800c-5ebb-aa61-1cb4d2210b07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259448}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "06d9e7d7-47d1-54b7-9b9c-2dd2d48786ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-124663" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{MN}$ traversed from the point $M(-1,1)$ to the point $N(1,3),$ then ${\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+{x}^{2}\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+j$ and $b=i+3\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =-i+j+t(i+3\\,j-(-i+j)) \\\\  & =-i+j+t(2\\,i+2\\,j) \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=2t-1$ and $y(t)=2t+1$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=2\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+{x}^{2}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t+1)^{2}\\cdot 2+(2t-1)^{2}\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{t}^{2}+4t+1)+(4{t}^{2}-4t+1)\\,dt \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}8{t}^{2}+2\\,dt \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}4{t}^{2}+1\\,dt \\\\  & =4{[\\frac{4{t}^{3}}{3}+t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4[\\frac{4}{3}+10]-4[0] \\\\  & =\\frac{28}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326759 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "346fd834-9f4b-5298-99e8-684dece5b13b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324393} {:answer/id #uuid "40fc053f-efcc-59ec-af1c-fef223bf76b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324495} {:answer/id #uuid "c90f25cc-5183-51bc-8ea0-dfe66b0a8547" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325713} {:answer/id #uuid "cd18a5c9-22f8-510d-b764-46c7b3cf4a87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325748} {:answer/id #uuid "f57bcf97-908f-5723-a9d1-77a0ae528e17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd18a5c9-22f8-510d-b764-46c7b3cf4a87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325748} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0b9a63a-36ef-528c-ae36-20b4778b17db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326506}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4234b69-bc6e-5b88-b148-08ea10478920" :question/math-academy-id "q-39345" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from the point $A(1,2)$ to the point $B(3,4),$ then ${\\int }_{C}x\\,dx+y\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+2\\,j$ and $b=3\\,i+4\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =i+2\\,j+t(3\\,i+4\\,j-(i+2\\,j)) \\\\  & =i+2\\,j+t(2\\,i+2\\,j) \\\\  & =(1+2t)\\,i+(2+2t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=1+2t$ and $y(t)=2+2t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=2\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x\\,dx+y\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+2t)\\cdot 2+(2+2t)\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6+8t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[6t+4{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[6(1)+4(1)^{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351218 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "499b11ec-b714-5e35-93a8-a00dd90bd487" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349115} {:answer/id #uuid "4b3d1df7-d187-5a76-9ff2-d586354e93f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349135} {:answer/id #uuid "a07e3936-f719-59ae-bec5-b46ca80359cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349872} {:answer/id #uuid "ce5f8cad-b0d3-57e8-8823-669fc1f82f6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350261} {:answer/id #uuid "dc675675-601c-5a06-9c0d-83e3763e65c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b3d1df7-d187-5a76-9ff2-d586354e93f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349135} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a5f8fc5-bd8c-52a6-b3a1-caea03d05cdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350917}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "88864edb-1c9c-588d-bdc6-1eef5e251f66" :question/math-academy-id "q-81102" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from the point $A(0,1)$ to the point $B(2,3),$ then ${\\int }_{C}{e}^{x}\\,dx-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{2})\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=j$ and $b=2\\,i+3\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =j+t(2\\,i+3\\,j-j) \\\\  & =j+t(2\\,i+2j) \\\\  & =2t\\,i+(1+2t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=2t$ and $y(t)=1+2t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=2\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x}\\,dx-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{2})\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{2})\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{x}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{2})\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t}\\cdot 2-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi (1+2t)}{2})\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{e}^{2t}-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi t))dt \\\\  & ={[{e}^{2t}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi t)]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[{e}^{2(1)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi (1))]-[{e}^{2(0)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi (0))] \\\\  & =[{e}^{2}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})]-[1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})] \\\\  & =[{e}^{2}+0]-[1+0] \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415441 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f77ceb7-6be8-5d0b-93e7-60790b6d14db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415407} {:answer/id #uuid "488a4e09-5667-54f6-ad88-5c61e9a4ef4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415411} {:answer/id #uuid "65db0f0e-cfd6-52b8-9117-b967fb489769" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415415} {:answer/id #uuid "8b52cac3-7234-5848-87cf-921935519eb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415421} {:answer/id #uuid "9b98bb58-d459-5b8d-8729-a3f1ab440861" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f77ceb7-6be8-5d0b-93e7-60790b6d14db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415407} :answer-field/id #uuid "79e92368-7b49-5e53-95cb-a87e9c528421" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415435}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "36f8b820-02e2-5d5e-8155-bd2e7c9da1db" :question/math-academy-id "q-124660" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to the point $B(1,1),$ then ${\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y\\,dx-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=0\\,i+0\\,j$ and $b=i+j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =0\\,i+0\\,j+t(i+j-(0\\,i+0\\,j)) \\\\  & =t\\,i+t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=t$ and $y(t)=t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y\\,dx-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\cdot 1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\cdot 1\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t}{\\pi }-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t}{\\pi }]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\pi }[-1+0-1-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415443 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29488f70-3dff-5945-99fa-114e41ce2085" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415405} {:answer/id #uuid "38c233c1-8522-530d-9bbc-48e152799452" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415409} {:answer/id #uuid "794f752f-1659-5318-a69d-58c5a353960e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415419} {:answer/id #uuid "d6e2e962-8b22-57c7-9fde-b02c03d0533e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415425} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ac4b86-8657-5c33-ab2c-ac749e9ddbc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415428}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38c233c1-8522-530d-9bbc-48e152799452" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415409} :answer-field/id #uuid "b523b71f-1a4d-563c-8ba4-de711d207cf0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415437}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b73ad7ec-dc24-5c99-a917-9ce5919adbcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-90714" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{FG}$ traversed from the point $F(1,0)$ to the point $G(2,3),$ then ${\\int }_{C}x\\,dx-xy\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i$ and $b=2\\,i+3\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =i+t(2\\,i+3\\,j-i) \\\\  & =i+t(i+3\\,j) \\\\  & =(1+t)\\,i+3t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=1+t$ and $y(t)=3t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+3\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x\\,dx-xy\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+t)\\cdot 1-(1+t)\\cdot 3t\\cdot 3\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}1-8t-9{t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[t-4{t}^{2}-3{t}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[1-4(1)^{2}-3(1)^{3}]-[0] \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Over Segments"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184148 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184055 :question/id #uuid "fdd0c8ab-6977-5a8f-b1db-37ae9b2b116e" :question/math-academy-id "e-3663" :question/problem "The graph below shows the function $y=\\cot{(Bx)}+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What are the values of $B$ and $D?$\n\n![](images/06/062083968cd64f10523e99e2dd4aecb405f28f781b8b0c194f11b6017943a801.png)" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is $\\dfrac \\pi 2.$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\cot(Bx + C) + D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{\\pi}{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac \\pi 2  = \\dfrac{\\pi}{B}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad B = \\dfrac{\\pi}{\\left(\\dfrac \\pi 2\\right)} = 2.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the tangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $1$ unit down. Therefore, $D=-1.$\n\nSo, the final equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\cot\\left(2x\\right)-1.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dac9c72b-c23f-56fa-b939-6e3b56d3a13b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184829 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3677d397-6d3e-51fa-89f2-0755957600d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333491} {:answer/id #uuid "374ffe77-8540-5fea-8b6a-93bc4dd5c6a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333497} {:answer/id #uuid "c4b8fc41-f6cf-57fc-a619-45a3d5194deb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334695} {:answer/id #uuid "c4dc2cec-17d7-5ee2-afad-2eaf8b6ecd3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334698} {:answer/id #uuid "fd657de6-624e-5f19-ac8b-2aa2527fd437" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd657de6-624e-5f19-ac8b-2aa2527fd437" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335218} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b4a0441-f374-5ba6-b7d7-22b89426a4ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335255}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "91496801-bb35-528a-8bb8-dd007b8e0c83" :question/math-academy-id "q-65549" :question/problem "![](images/25/25cdbb7ea69245986f5fc3df65490aa6ca72d0adcf319a768c5243d7b692e3d8.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B-2D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{5}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{5}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{\\pi }{5})}=5.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $\\frac{3}{2}$ units up. Hence, $D=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, $B-2D=5-2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2})=5-3=2.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5x)+\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184844 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aad4bfcf-ac83-5c47-928b-5b1a7fc0f104" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183674} {:answer/id #uuid "df3e3991-8f50-5de8-b75b-5960efe17e47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183675} {:answer/id #uuid "f2572169-df3d-5c99-975a-9f3f678ee3a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183676} {:answer/id #uuid "f28c8896-b9fe-5342-9cba-cebeee74006f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183677} {:answer/id #uuid "095c622d-5db2-5a48-942e-b312abb3f7c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183678}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2d980f41-06e1-5562-995c-dd718d6a8c7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183673} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "081bfb29-fde1-5481-b123-8eac743f086f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399099} {:answer/id #uuid "51e08aac-9339-503c-9a66-b74268d4d2f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399100} {:answer/id #uuid "03fedd1c-29b4-5722-b1e7-b3c046e5b66c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399101} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ce8598-f6eb-5e70-b2e5-0d95b7a5f429" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399102} {:answer/id #uuid "6704d952-af2f-5f8a-a722-c90281d08d85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03fedd1c-29b4-5722-b1e7-b3c046e5b66c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399101} :answer-field/id #uuid "1848e56b-8e02-510d-9f75-0f28e4f3c082" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399098}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d92d6b12-34ec-5e74-a8fa-e75755bbf55a" :question/math-academy-id "q-79188" :question/problem "![](images/ae/ae6a514e0d9cde3db3c3b1dfa94e3804b5469dd5fb99b40a94684e88b573ec38.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}=3.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $3$ units up. Hence, $D=3.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=3+3=6.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)+3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253413 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ec68e9c-38e6-559a-a887-a8091041ac37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250683} {:answer/id #uuid "4d694e8b-7771-57ad-9c4a-86d77c9ded0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251195} {:answer/id #uuid "58dfdc7e-2c25-5c4d-b4d1-2a6e2b08606c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251283} {:answer/id #uuid "afb9e314-7855-5834-b616-8d65e3144e61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252086} {:answer/id #uuid "fc68c657-d12f-59d8-bfa1-b90e0b393d10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58dfdc7e-2c25-5c4d-b4d1-2a6e2b08606c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251283} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6f51877-7815-5bb3-9f0e-484d066099ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253175}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b0fa25dc-6bee-5930-931e-86bd1acea862" :question/math-academy-id "q-117798" :question/problem "![](images/45/454c2746d17f6444a9fa5762baae3927fb72eff6a7e51a1df38bc4f325a4a830.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-0=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $2$ units up. Hence, $D=2.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=\\frac{3}{2}+2=\\frac{7}{2}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2})+2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253414 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "235fd882-5110-59ee-a1e5-8d11855075da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250856} {:answer/id #uuid "7622bb9f-c5f3-5f32-969e-e995445640b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251524} {:answer/id #uuid "8c81d441-7f34-5323-9426-fda6851b2d9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251739} {:answer/id #uuid "d578743c-119d-54fb-8e3f-7d59d8673902" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252445} {:answer/id #uuid "f2fbad45-0af4-5538-9c2d-b46d7e2b12d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d578743c-119d-54fb-8e3f-7d59d8673902" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252445} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebfa8d92-cd88-5898-b22c-46f756efc46d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253217}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "15fef2c4-3c6f-5cf8-99dc-a49b56e65469" :question/math-academy-id "q-117800" :question/problem "![](images/60/6003a85e0038b7e6562381cd338a7597877d1152adbeb476b7141eee74ba06ee.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}-0=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\pi }{5}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{2\\pi }{5})}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $\\frac{5}{4}$ units up. Hence, $D=\\frac{5}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{5}{4}=\\frac{15}{4}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2})+\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275123 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d117b4c-f410-531d-89d3-d240d2770c1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272501} {:answer/id #uuid "4d8916c1-d8cc-539d-8500-05fc10ff1e66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273036} {:answer/id #uuid "79c5cd52-cb65-55b4-acff-be2a9c77bec7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273433} {:answer/id #uuid "bc4377ee-1f77-5413-9810-dbe2141bcc16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{17}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274016} {:answer/id #uuid "e30fc5a2-00c2-5877-9a02-34776a22449c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79c5cd52-cb65-55b4-acff-be2a9c77bec7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273433} :answer-field/id #uuid "5721fa62-48d4-5b2c-87d7-f6231add9a93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274783}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "39eb902a-b87c-5ca3-bc9b-ac7146c8f3fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-143665" :question/problem "![](images/39/39d3bd73ac0f60f21cc9e71cbf46d62901fcc07cab3b06cd84a496b1e1415a1a.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $5$ units down. Hence, $D=-5.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=\\frac{3}{2}+(-5)=-\\frac{7}{2}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2})-5.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275124 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ad738c6-8a32-5961-b539-3698f4e9cdf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273021} {:answer/id #uuid "50259aca-1ec1-5fc2-bc96-3ca3f1858648" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273065} {:answer/id #uuid "71aa11bf-864b-5f96-989e-b92b337377a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273363} {:answer/id #uuid "dee2a307-86c3-5f67-84cc-ac5b0ed4f0f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274351} {:answer/id #uuid "ff038ed4-d456-5bbd-b444-2bac21ae9a18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274629}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dee2a307-86c3-5f67-84cc-ac5b0ed4f0f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274351} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9af95c9-9911-57b3-8b64-ba864ef27435" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274934}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "24086e6f-d631-5bda-bb74-7b003ee42cee" :question/math-academy-id "q-143666" :question/problem "![](images/14/148ef6b6d54262df159703437a6003e8f884052b98e95eb61536890a987a07f1.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B-D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{4\\pi }{5}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{4\\pi }{5}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{4\\pi }{5})}=\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $\\frac{9}{4}$ units up. Hence, $D=\\frac{9}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, $B-D=\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{9}{4}=-1.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{5x}{4})+\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275125 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b569561-dd6a-56fe-9bd4-68e9ac673886" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273170} {:answer/id #uuid "6ee36efa-131f-5155-a5b2-c1679185c3dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273350} {:answer/id #uuid "a268efae-7964-5246-bfea-d43fd33e4816" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273793} {:answer/id #uuid "c4e6e28d-d15c-52b3-aa11-573250fde94a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274093} {:answer/id #uuid "e92cf54e-2ca8-575a-a3ad-f3bb68f26116" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ee36efa-131f-5155-a5b2-c1679185c3dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273350} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8f9e0d5-a165-5d96-b7ec-3b0afc3b50ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274983}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bc1adc45-4e2f-5dc9-9f7c-79b8e4bcae44" :question/math-academy-id "q-143668" :question/problem "![](images/f6/f6ee30d5c189c9579a1f38d59f06581a7cfff944d42489eab2e3c0c6ce089ad4.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{5\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})}=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $\\frac{12}{5}$ units down. Hence, $D=-\\frac{12}{5}.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=\\frac{3}{5}+(-\\frac{12}{5})=-\\frac{9}{5}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3x}{5})-\\frac{12}{5}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398172 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd86b80a-f69a-5bee-9e25-9498124a3b56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398174} {:answer/id #uuid "ae4cbf13-3436-5ca9-a85e-d79666e97f91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398175} {:answer/id #uuid "a192845d-5444-593f-a294-b8369a05e099" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398176} {:answer/id #uuid "e570139b-a17c-5e71-8fdc-ac883bda1ab2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398177} {:answer/id #uuid "c337dc59-1dd1-5197-98b9-3c6fcbf08e62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a192845d-5444-593f-a294-b8369a05e099" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398176} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b387afd-4a67-578b-8db3-0662089f213d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398173}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "554b2923-f03c-51dd-9817-6e0a2f8280b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-117799" :question/problem "![](images/c9/c9ed24a7fa07d6c29e4125c8428fb61a42fc969b99f101c4737bdedf9bf036ff.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}-0=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{4\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $3$ units down. Hence, $D=-3.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=\\frac{3}{4}+(-3)=-\\frac{9}{4}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3x}{4})-3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398466 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "accd213e-a18c-57f9-a2cf-0e3297615ffe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398468} {:answer/id #uuid "bd8269e6-822d-57d9-a7d5-81b77feea90f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398469} {:answer/id #uuid "b6d90684-6641-5156-b37c-32b02b1bdd9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398470} {:answer/id #uuid "21d7fa3c-5da8-50fa-8d80-231c8289856a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398471} {:answer/id #uuid "70cad25b-cabf-5869-8148-7ca6a521ce0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd8269e6-822d-57d9-a7d5-81b77feea90f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398469} :answer-field/id #uuid "412e6109-4d77-5424-95b6-1257add5ac9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398467}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b85652aa-105e-56e5-bee4-742b27b9d838" :question/math-academy-id "q-141999" :question/problem "![](images/8b/8b237f9e857b1ed87de447aa79c14d359429e71d8afe7ab8eab201eba7ffb6af.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is  $B+2D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=2.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $1$ unit up. Therefore, $D=1.$\n\nTherefore, $B+2D=2+2\\cdot (1)=2+2=4.$  \n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)+1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398473 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1477d75c-69cc-56f6-83aa-951c0dfbc5a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398475} {:answer/id #uuid "d1a7f65b-0eec-54b0-bd1b-1489777d7346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398476} {:answer/id #uuid "251fc1e5-eecc-5524-8fcc-50a9a87bac4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398477} {:answer/id #uuid "c3a9476a-83e1-5900-adda-60bacbc12a44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398478} {:answer/id #uuid "823522a9-4039-541d-b6e1-6b18113ea496" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398479}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3a9476a-83e1-5900-adda-60bacbc12a44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398478} :answer-field/id #uuid "25043d55-6e38-5a47-9ce8-35564e623dc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398474}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "949bd398-6573-53d2-bf2d-21cf0dde3f19" :question/math-academy-id "q-142000" :question/problem "![](images/98/98edb404419e7ab515109f87addb44709513007b1a0105b648a3ce2b62ad547f.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}=3.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $1$ unit up. Hence, $D=1.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=3+1=4.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)+1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398480 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f9abc86-74e1-5d19-8f4f-0f0e7a56d821" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398482} {:answer/id #uuid "ab992999-a43c-5b0c-874e-f97040b58cc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398483} {:answer/id #uuid "d0aa5750-a42b-5232-a712-34850cd9b3f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398484} {:answer/id #uuid "91869c0b-2488-53b8-980c-0c2c402ce0f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398485} {:answer/id #uuid "9e0d1de6-b1c8-5a40-bc71-a155680b07fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e0d1de6-b1c8-5a40-bc71-a155680b07fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398486} :answer-field/id #uuid "a33bc7f4-158e-59b4-b555-27008daa9757" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398481}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d39218d1-1972-5152-bee8-516235be3a28" :question/math-academy-id "q-143669" :question/problem "![](images/f8/f86984919b15283134e1b3a9479ff8c79ec8dfacc84975e82f88704b7b26dd3a.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is  $B-2D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}=4.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $\\frac{9}{4}$ unit down. Therefore, $D=-\\frac{9}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, $B-2D=4-2\\cdot (-\\frac{9}{4})=4+\\frac{9}{2}=\\frac{17}{2}.$  \n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x)-\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398926 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c00c5e3a-5697-52dc-aa31-3d95907996f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398928} {:answer/id #uuid "1cfeda65-26b6-5502-a718-3aca8a4514b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398929} {:answer/id #uuid "4633095b-b665-59ce-aeaa-96dbb9f25eb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398930} {:answer/id #uuid "f41004c5-d81d-5e7e-bb0b-5fc9c65c979d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398931} {:answer/id #uuid "919cc779-2edd-5c5e-b2ef-d5ab24d25505" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "919cc779-2edd-5c5e-b2ef-d5ab24d25505" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398932} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd0dd138-ee92-58b5-a97c-038d65811dd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398927}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a31794b-90ed-5858-8a23-bda34267af75" :question/math-academy-id "q-65550" :question/problem "![](images/c9/c904a1eb628e726647a6f655a567a0939f7b7d404f0b32c6c4b4adaeca847916.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B-D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{4\\pi }{5}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{4\\pi }{5}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{4\\pi }{5})}=\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $3$ units down. Hence, $D=-3.$\n\nTherefore, $B-D=(\\frac{5}{4})-(-3)=\\frac{5}{4}+3=\\frac{17}{4}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{5x}{4})-3.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Stretch and Vertical Shift of a Transformed Cotangent Curve"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184149 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184056 :question/id #uuid "90986bdd-15dd-51c6-b5b0-4174542736c9" :question/math-academy-id "e-3669" :question/problem "![](images/5a/5a58c1b6b72ab29c73457834bd093b23df234d4b161d87b026449ce18a510cb6.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function ![](images/25/2590787ae9eed509fa2eca140078f34dc0263e5d888cb50ef81bb317896f4fa7.png) where ![](images/4a/4a4b94629aa74d8b04caecd666b2b34f6d7a4db03b561a0207102093f3ce0366.png) is a constant. Given that ![](images/f7/f75fd972834cbdd20e3329eee1406c25476109f66bdc63b50e65d43b495416c8.png) what is the value of ![](images/05/0514151a4defc8b3e97ec86ead73038697701ead4a5999db5c16affffd20c41f.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parameter ![](images/04/04d3d028f9f3766f1e44fb4a095a62dc92a9f16259ac8ee930254e2da0e52ae0.png) represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n![](images/23/23f74abe2b793b6e5d090136cafe331c5f92d4ff62df66ec94b75539259b5a6e.png)\n\nThe factor ![](images/25/2500722483533ba6dd622e9572f36ef0162889a6801352ce8f2c60f60c55167e.png) indicates that the graph of ![](images/dd/dd80268d579d79a6f0ca99485ec391b4034877f1f14a52fa9e6cd371cf2bfaee.png) is a horizontal translation of ![](images/f3/f324f716f57c696de3669f03ba36b1aa197bed1c36144777252490084c940344.png) Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/4a/4ae5b54c0b0aafdb3e191dd01d0c3ff698bda9daee3a51ad49ecf0a0313c8186.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting ![](images/a8/a878f9eac36b11ec0a6ac3ba961c0257a9e73e872127c0167d9b64554acc84b6.png) by ![](images/b7/b7bbd8a714eb95c5081e7511b5b003ead8d98e0d38de8dc061d82ac15a486752.png) units to the right. Therefore,\n\n![](images/3b/3bcb5d5f6e6988266fedfb0cc507aa46fdbf510e2d15ca8588253228af091621.png)\n\nSince ![](images/9c/9c5ec1702df206d7f81e68ea6fbc5b795335b0b9317a4540446fe5c4235aa0bc.png) and ![](images/65/65f6e2f05e1d05f16a4f6601ebe3e49f4cb01663d31807feb5de5f6bb9e2331e.png) is in this interval, the value of ![](images/b5/b5c9da4fbe9c1486e2080416f6a3044a790267f9eb072ef34c164e5e12958e5c.png) is ![](images/ee/eef2c2e86e460a67d52eee56af496164737b41a0e2db4c1e6d4a4030b4c00167.png)."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84355770-5926-5be6-9b9f-c13bb05599ae" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184830 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35078d74-667f-5d42-9d17-366be03c50b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333475} {:answer/id #uuid "a68ca484-4871-5d33-a32d-9c7dd67ecbc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334449} {:answer/id #uuid "d31b1ea6-9889-5698-98e1-878acc489e07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334837} {:answer/id #uuid "e2dd2f79-e39a-58bc-aee5-01f3ef542729" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334963} {:answer/id #uuid "fc5bd80c-b257-5028-b120-1e6934062109" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc5bd80c-b257-5028-b120-1e6934062109" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335207} :answer-field/id #uuid "50abe121-f986-569c-96f5-0b825aa3ad90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335396}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "397bffc1-1dbf-51bd-b606-1b9fd6b7e36a" :question/math-academy-id "q-65570" :question/problem "![](images/4a/4afd6e9f2b3343a8364f088653aa99cc4ceeeeef7c53f9014b2dd83314f66118.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}(x+2C))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+2C$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}(x+2C))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=tan\\,(\\frac{x}{2}).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/8a/8a19beac86998c845d7ffa225a52d425ab48b9ec350f6c8fe42f7c6dbce027ce.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ units to the left. Therefore:\n\n$$\n2C=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{8}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184845 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25efffe0-a97f-5bba-872f-819835e25610" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345695} {:answer/id #uuid "2d97bafb-5a87-5ba7-b763-927943b3e7cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345755} {:answer/id #uuid "51ef6fff-f3d4-57ee-b1a4-d5aada807fc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346056} {:answer/id #uuid "b12badcd-f45e-5665-aa07-1cbd839e3523" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346899} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf326fc-b3ea-5f91-bf0c-6759d86b03a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddf326fc-b3ea-5f91-bf0c-6759d86b03a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347302} :answer-field/id #uuid "1fc3c1af-059f-504a-97e2-7584a4073b63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347659}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6c0fa171-9c01-5949-8df7-ed01d22c88ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-79194" :question/problem "![](images/a4/a41a721eab2eb97e8b7b30ddf877e37a48d2b74eeef82ab0a4b72f3bb4a93603.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2(x+\\frac{C}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{2}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2(x+\\frac{C}{2}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=tan\\,(2x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/f0/f08846ce1f79cd14c593fbf37360fb8169a26704c956506ca0deedb8025e3b39.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253425 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1382a0da-6af2-58cd-a188-1d157bd20cd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250722} {:answer/id #uuid "255f49c4-fc05-50ed-8b6d-c7d105680f5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250875} {:answer/id #uuid "70d6cf7f-09b3-54e7-91e5-21216ebc3505" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251475} {:answer/id #uuid "8f7c0e22-c4ef-5f16-9e47-d3a2ab3e0908" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251770} {:answer/id #uuid "958902e2-d4fc-583d-abca-b3667fecc3aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "958902e2-d4fc-583d-abca-b3667fecc3aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251838} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dd471e5-3d02-59ea-ae43-9700669b6052" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252832}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3de18967-1c79-524e-b070-80260fcd64fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-117888" :question/problem "![](images/58/5819e0605dacfb190c2767c4f50a671eb592832aa6a3a1970a8bcdff2790fd19.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{3}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/ad/ad5bf0001a37cf5bebf3288ffbf1fa6bb69747f28f8a81d08d0ffab8223e1707.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{21}-0=\\frac{\\pi }{21}$ units to the right. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{3}=-\\frac{\\pi }{21}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{7}.\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $-\\frac{\\pi }{7}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253426 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08bb1537-d88e-5b3f-911a-ebda185d71d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250631} {:answer/id #uuid "154d1a5b-94de-5332-826d-f7085ae1bbac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250735} {:answer/id #uuid "6982de92-482e-5773-8de9-08b335a18349" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251422} {:answer/id #uuid "8612ead4-47dc-5874-b6e3-9dfd76a3c1d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251675} {:answer/id #uuid "ad9bf3b6-8f0d-5e32-8900-ae60d369c4d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252066}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "154d1a5b-94de-5332-826d-f7085ae1bbac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250735} :answer-field/id #uuid "5303117c-9cc9-5aa9-a8ac-5b23f63b8352" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252952}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3e0561fe-63de-5941-8751-b8579e1f5296" :question/math-academy-id "q-117890" :question/problem "![](images/d5/d54c590d8d64670c0fb90baea864d154cb4c59cd69d7f57234bfd8a5c4a3bfe7.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{4}(x+4C))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+4C$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{4}(x+4C))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/33/336c38a44a3a40fcaf4a226f61a9f7af2cc731674c1ccc6679b80d846d4ea657.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})$ by $0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ units to the left. Therefore\n\n$$\n4C=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{8}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253427 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bcb0260-5769-5c20-88aa-a4f01f0ed3e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251052} {:answer/id #uuid "605d6a7a-183a-572b-88b6-7f2f4255e6ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251341} {:answer/id #uuid "645de680-5d7f-5ab0-a739-68065e200b35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251374} {:answer/id #uuid "9b5d31a5-d46b-5fe9-a177-ab14a5602ff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251890} {:answer/id #uuid "f1a9237f-441e-59ec-bbfc-da0afdb86299" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "605d6a7a-183a-572b-88b6-7f2f4255e6ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251341} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8963d4e-4e20-5bae-a6bb-d591908baaca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253125}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "68a48b4f-4833-5c9d-96bb-4f430bff0952" :question/math-academy-id "q-117891" :question/problem "![](images/43/4374d7ede23ffbb78ed861d8ebdf116126c546490a87771505cfbe121b973247.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2(x+\\frac{C}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{2}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2(x+\\frac{C}{2}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/ca/cac3ea6b8409290b6716a194325a885672e06572c1f8a8b63b48b6807f514d42.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ units to the left. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275126 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27dd6c2e-56f6-5224-a92d-a900e47fbf5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272740} {:answer/id #uuid "4d20665a-d072-5d38-bc2e-83cd6ab4dfd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273035} {:answer/id #uuid "69b8a942-cc3f-5887-9b9a-88358ec6410d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273298} {:answer/id #uuid "a7e2122f-6484-54b6-b0a8-b983751ab3ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273841} {:answer/id #uuid "dc531f8c-0ec3-5820-905b-629562f0fa99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27dd6c2e-56f6-5224-a92d-a900e47fbf5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272740} :answer-field/id #uuid "b21fc2a6-944a-50a1-a0fa-69bde4da9e7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274944}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bdfc61bf-0fdc-5699-9c08-0b5a229a8b71" :question/math-academy-id "q-143670" :question/problem "![](images/c2/c2e5b01cf03f1f9d82562ee51fc484448e90b74dd2584daf18a499849ca36bff.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}(x+3C))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+3C$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}(x+3C))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/29/29346a073d6a22f0bab74eeac86431e8c761c9ed21a005ec6965f71e6436c117.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})$ by $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n3C=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{12}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $-\\frac{\\pi }{12}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275127 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31a8036f-17ff-55a2-804a-9b2f935b3eb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272833} {:answer/id #uuid "872ba577-0336-51a3-a330-83ebd6f756ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273545} {:answer/id #uuid "9b9681ec-7818-50c5-b8e6-16783c330ca1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273732} {:answer/id #uuid "c7309cde-90ce-5d0e-b0e2-2d1526e4f905" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274117} {:answer/id #uuid "fd64378f-da19-5467-a8d4-3c79f815b9c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd64378f-da19-5467-a8d4-3c79f815b9c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274615} :answer-field/id #uuid "20a1e721-de4b-5fc5-9f28-757e2e6b70a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274694}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "64482e47-1429-5ccc-90cd-b783347aa71c" :question/math-academy-id "q-143671" :question/problem "![](images/b2/b29154c183a57397bba6050ad02e543ad47e4b979c77435c9f6e88a49e32783e.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2(x+\\frac{C}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{2}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2(x+\\frac{C}{2}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/59/595222a0a6987c1fae1a7b245d2936eba08312357015cc6f78c66eb60f942707.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)$ by $\\frac{5\\pi }{12}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275128 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fb8c6d5-62f2-5f9e-9dff-a6c6cf210773" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272526} {:answer/id #uuid "3d8111fb-6012-56e0-a9b6-3275417d9a31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272916} {:answer/id #uuid "8340b495-0e27-5ab2-8265-89b8a8f42f44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273514} {:answer/id #uuid "ceceedd5-348a-5f86-9a42-9b4e036292b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274185} {:answer/id #uuid "ed397ac8-10b1-54c0-9046-d661bdad85ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d8111fb-6012-56e0-a9b6-3275417d9a31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272916} :answer-field/id #uuid "b42dc950-3ab6-53ee-bb79-c0cd1d57e61e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274946}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "587d6141-4c7d-5ff0-9ea2-3cc64d30a411" :question/math-academy-id "q-143672" :question/problem "![](images/6a/6a90f799d2dfd2dbd971e15eb195f4109ab7b6ceb204943a8ed2fd6c83bb620c.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}(x+2C))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+2C$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}(x+2C))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=cot\\,(\\frac{x}{2}).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/0e/0e94fbec60ae4d47d3a4fba50f8a875d9bb3938776940cc9688413d2cb22aa9e.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ units to the left. Therefore:\n\n$$\n2C=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275129 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07c6a391-2c92-5813-ba67-4345a362e8af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272454} {:answer/id #uuid "4770e5d3-4587-5515-9152-939b65d37b5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272997} {:answer/id #uuid "5cbb6915-a1c8-5fc7-a06e-af95c679745f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273184} {:answer/id #uuid "88b96039-922d-5b50-8541-b48f2cbcf322" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273564} {:answer/id #uuid "8ab7af29-dc13-5490-8ec4-2b37032e3d65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4770e5d3-4587-5515-9152-939b65d37b5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272997} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b65d7ea-f125-581a-a2df-c1111f4277a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274847}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6ae3e439-d759-5b28-9539-614b9c6d1865" :question/math-academy-id "q-143674" :question/problem "![](images/ee/ee4ad78545160a052a28f0f0fcc7df476cc2063e48429a315a9cb9242f7d5311.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{3}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/e1/e1ed3a322c82b5c70acb01c54c97b09814f41aec80d980034a6fcdc46037209c.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{12}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ units to the left. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275130 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24bcd966-4d0a-58d0-943e-8cf4e9c4e1ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272709} {:answer/id #uuid "6674d189-d413-5051-9da8-54c2f39014d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273265} {:answer/id #uuid "a0591c4c-0b63-5e56-a640-94f5dfe3dd67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273775} {:answer/id #uuid "cb64e6cc-c9ba-5473-982d-43077a936e0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274156} {:answer/id #uuid "d7774e07-5f8a-5b18-ad81-f5efc8beffbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7774e07-5f8a-5b18-ad81-f5efc8beffbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274272} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f5fe593-1589-5c40-aac0-bfffbafcf416" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274743}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c12af7ec-d535-5bd8-818a-db72ffd5471c" :question/math-academy-id "q-143675" :question/problem "![](images/a8/a8fbbaff1139a0c80c5dc97a9d99a08e238a61ab65251a158b382e208be7e842.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4(x+\\frac{C}{4}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{4}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4(x+\\frac{C}{4}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/4c/4c5147d95cc4d5df1d847f5fe9f580d9595d0f4d8c2c8209efa1f2a3cd9c5aea.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x)$ by $\\frac{9\\pi }{40}-\\frac{\\pi }{8}=\\frac{\\pi }{10}$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{4}=-\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{2\\pi }{5}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275131 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03bc168c-f11d-51c2-bcec-43e2f2c594d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272418} {:answer/id #uuid "1d2a9ebc-fa26-5fa6-bfe6-f8b5ba052e2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272646} {:answer/id #uuid "24d9abd2-8f95-581f-a159-19f44168c652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272710} {:answer/id #uuid "af303134-44b2-56cd-975a-36c2a314c7d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273901} {:answer/id #uuid "d30c5ce1-6aae-525e-9853-2bf0d012d50c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af303134-44b2-56cd-975a-36c2a314c7d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273901} :answer-field/id #uuid "0189c09e-e677-5254-a0fb-54a417d40114" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274641}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b8a997a9-7fce-538b-be7b-e8029e694287" :question/math-academy-id "q-143676" :question/problem "![](images/0f/0f994407d304e5b1105aeef37a62d6d51bcad43e76757846e0b42b8f257024b3.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2x}{5}+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2x}{5}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2}{5}(x+\\frac{5C}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{5C}{2}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2}{5}(x+\\frac{5C}{2}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2x}{5}).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/1b/1b3e3365d8047befbe70c2371a44e61262c68a2da694ab410ae974917e8535bd.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2x}{5})$ by $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}-\\frac{5\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5C}{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,C=(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})(\\frac{2}{5})\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{5}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $-\\frac{\\pi }{5}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275132 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38f5df54-de20-581a-a052-687662bac099" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272886} {:answer/id #uuid "59ea7cd8-2c89-5af6-9951-0c58c07e0a6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273157} {:answer/id #uuid "98bf869f-24f8-50f3-ac5e-4060bb11e000" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273712} {:answer/id #uuid "b3d9faeb-5941-52e8-928f-04d747d92a64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273945} {:answer/id #uuid "c58db38f-c8f1-5754-b80e-2298972c7447" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274101}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59ea7cd8-2c89-5af6-9951-0c58c07e0a6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273157} :answer-field/id #uuid "20a99701-dbbe-534f-a0ce-166bf09e58cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274695}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7239e789-03d0-5ec3-8e95-e5eb72761b2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-143677" :question/problem "![](images/76/7602d1695610361f28613d8bf3542056b2624f9b6b8980873f6fdebf802c6750.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3}+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}(x+\\frac{3C}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{3C}{2}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}(x+\\frac{3C}{2}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3}).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/01/015ca9336646a7545de842414ace2c2a695688e30653b2ed019000d69609d56d.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3})$ by $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ units to the left. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3C}{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,⟹\\,C=(\\frac{\\pi }{4})(\\frac{2}{3})\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335845 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21e5506f-4577-5036-ada5-dc6f9bc99169" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333292} {:answer/id #uuid "366585d6-d0fc-50e1-b496-79591f6c5405" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333490} {:answer/id #uuid "75f6de9c-83fb-55b9-9419-ef8f92a5ae22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334056} {:answer/id #uuid "e198d2ca-23db-5649-85de-6116093fd7d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334955} {:answer/id #uuid "ee45bb2e-4cfb-5586-96b6-963f79e9e559" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "366585d6-d0fc-50e1-b496-79591f6c5405" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333490} :answer-field/id #uuid "10496ff6-7779-5051-aba6-5bba4e7b9c2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335269}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "baa94592-3dad-5374-a846-3b63aecad625" :question/math-academy-id "q-65571" :question/problem "![](images/2f/2f11d5b11c53f7b826291e0b7e95828f57d6f1242b768baf12c874be229b2935.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3x}{5}+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3x}{5}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{5}(x+\\frac{5C}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{5C}{3}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{5}(x+\\frac{5C}{3}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3x}{5}).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/5e/5e9b6568ec493d95db6fe72a6a19024326c5b920629e9ff44a85db54089aa2a6.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3x}{5})$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{3}-0=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5C}{3}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,⟹\\,C=(-\\frac{\\pi }{3})(\\frac{3}{5})\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{5}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $-\\frac{\\pi }{5}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348171 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2452d0e1-4362-5796-a78d-dd02a6585ca8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345683} {:answer/id #uuid "7cf12949-3c55-5003-9dd7-df558ba34624" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346441} {:answer/id #uuid "b0f87ce7-dd80-5709-99d0-ca3b27881647" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346896} {:answer/id #uuid "bbebdb1c-5559-5ac4-b7a7-c46f4dd61296" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346983} {:answer/id #uuid "f0deae7a-65de-5629-be07-d8599eee87d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cf12949-3c55-5003-9dd7-df558ba34624" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346441} :answer-field/id #uuid "8571455f-3add-56cd-b95f-65d5a2eb936b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347840}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "83cb784b-d860-58fe-8b2f-7d0321406fb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-79195" :question/problem "![](images/89/89efc20f1c6527d1bcd501f97ce585fb5a60e9791804f56d61f33653cf1a70d7.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{3}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/f8/f8617da98ea9d9fb48b8fef588b624c6961a299233836fe433c1819b697ca0fb.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)$ by $0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{12})=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ units to the left. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Shift of a Horizontally Stretched Tangent or Cotangent Curve"}]}
